    "short": "She sells tomatoes... She steals destiny. Ama Mensah 22, poor tomato seller at Kaneshie, watches customers leave her for Madam Darkoa whose tomatoes glow green and never spoil. Her blind grandmother shouts: This is not tomato, this is destiny!",
    "long": "Kaneshie Market, 4am. Ama Mensah, 22, beautiful hardworking tomato seller from Agbogbloshie, feeds her blind grandmother Grandma Esi and jobless brother Kofi. When strange old woman Madam Darkoa appears selling glowing red tomatoes that never spoil, Ama's business dies. Blind Grandma Esi touches one and screams it is destiny theft - each tomato is a trapped human star. 99 destinies already trapped, Ama will be number 100. With Mallam Musah's black soap and white handkerchief, Ama must sell the cursed tomato back to the Market Witch on Tuesday full market day. Green lightning, yellow glowing eyes, blood inside tomato - Ghanaian spiritual folk tale about envy, hard work and destiny. Pure Ghana names, Kaneshie, Agbogbloshie, Makola. Moral: Not every shining business is clean.",
    "characters": [{"name": "AMA MENSAH", "role": "Character"}, {"name": "GRANDMA ESI", "role": "Character"}, {"name": "KOFI", "role": "Character"}, {"name": "MADAM DARKOA", "role": "Character"}, {"name": "MALLAM MUSAH", "role": "Character"}, {"name": "AFIA", "role": "Character"}, {"name": "QUEEN YAA", "role": "Character"}, {"name": "MR JOSEPH", "role": "Character"}],
    "full": "NARRATOR: Kaneshie Market, 4am, dark, cold, only torch lights. Ama Mensah, 22 years, beautiful, hardworking, very poor tomato seller, carries heavy basket on head from Agbogbloshie. She walks alone to feed her blind grandmother Grandma Esi and her brother Kofi who is trotro driver but jobless.\n\nAMA MENSAH: Grandma Esi, today I will sell all my tomatoes by God's grace. I am tired of poverty. Kofi trotro no work, you blind, I am the only one. But I will not steal, I will not do juju. I will work hard with clean hands.\n\nGRANDMA ESI: My granddaughter Ama, my spirit is not comfortable today. I am blind with eyes but I see with spirit. Not all red is tomato. Not all shining market is clean. Some traders sell more than goods. They sell destiny. Be careful in Kaneshie today, my child.\n\nNARRATOR: Same market, same row, Madam Darkoa, 53 years, The Market Witch, strange woman. No one knows where she comes from, no family. She sells reddest, biggest, beautiful tomatoes. Her stall always dark even afternoon. Her tomatoes glow green at night and never spoil for 2 months. Customers start leaving Ama.\n\nAMA MENSAH: Why customers no dey buy from me today? My tomatoes fresh from Agbogbloshie this morning. I wake 4am. But everyone goes to Madam Darkoa. Her tomatoes never spoil? How? Something is wrong.\n\nAFIA: Ama, make I try buy one from Madam Darkoa and see. Maybe her own get chemical? She says her own stays 2 months without fridge.\n\nGRANDMA ESI: AHHHH! This is not tomato! This is destiny! This is human star! She is stealing people's stars! Afia, where you buy this evil thing? Throw it away!\n\nGRANDMA ESI: That woman is a market witch! In 1973, she was cursed by Nana Okomfo for using 3 children for money rituals at Tano River. The curse said she will never die, she will live by stealing other people's destiny. When you eat her tomato, she exchanges your good luck for her bad luck. Your market will die, your womb will close, your husband will leave you, your hair will fall, your money will vanish.\n\nAFIA: Too late Grandma Esi! I ate yesterday! I made stew! My boyfriend broke up this morning, my hair falling when I comb, my small savings GHC 200 disappeared! I am finished!\n\nAMA MENSAH: I must confront her! Tonight midnight at Kaneshie when market empty! No one will steal my destiny!\n\nNARRATOR: Midnight, 12:05am, empty Kaneshie Market, dogs barking far, only wind. Ama hides behind Queen Yaa stall. She sees Madam Darkoa sitting alone in middle of her dark stall, holding one tomato floating in air, with green lightning inside, chanting.\n\nMADAM DARKOA: Give me your market... give me your marriage... give me your children... give me your womb... give me your money... I give you tomatoes...\n\nMADAM DARKOA: You small girl... you want to know my secret? Come closer. Buy one tomato. Just one. One tomato for your whole future.\n\nNARRATOR: Ama runs away with fear, heart beating like drum. She runs straight to Makola to Mallam Musah, 80 years old mallam, who knows about market witches.\n\nMALLAM MUSAH: Eii, Ama Mensah, your destiny star already dim. You touched her stall last week when you borrowed knife. She started taking small small. Your customers reducing, body itching at night, dreaming of old woman? That is her hand. Take black soap and white handkerchief blessed at Mecca. Don't eat anything in market for 7 days. Don't take money with left hand. Bring me one glowing tomato at 12 midnight on 7th day.\n\nAMA MENSAH: I will do it Mallam. I will fast, I will pray. Save me and my grandmother.\n\nNARRATOR: 7th day, midnight, heavy rain, thunder. Ama with white handkerchief over head, steals one tomato from Madam Darkoa basket when Madam Darkoa goes to urinate behind stall. Tomato heavy like stone, cold like ice block, inside Ama hears baby crying, woman crying, man shouting for help.\n\nAMA MENSAH: Eiii! This tomato heavy! Cold! I hear baby crying inside! Mallam, what is this? Blood comes out, not water! Fresh human blood!\n\nMALLAM MUSAH: Ama, look! Each tomato is trapped destiny! This woman crying, she was tomato seller at Makola, lost all market. This young girl can't marry for 5 years. This man now mad at Lapaz. Madam Darkoa trapped 99 destinies. You will be number 100 and she will become young again, 22 years like you, and you will become old like her and die!\n\nNARRATOR: Meanwhile Kofi, Ama brother, 24 years, trotro driver, jobless, greedy, jealous of Ama small success, hears about Madam Darkoa quick money from friends at drinking spot.\n\nKOFI: Madam Darkoa, make me rich. I hear you give money quick. I will bring you my sister. She is beautiful, her star bright.\n\nMADAM DARKOA: Bring Ama hair from her sponge, and her pant from drying line. And bring her picture. I will make you rich in one night.\n\nKOFI: Forgive me sister, I need money for trotro.\n\nAMA MENSAH: Dreaming, woman with green hands strangling her inside her own stall. Ah! Leave me! Madam Darkoa, leave me!\n\nMALLAM MUSAH: Ama, only one way left. No more time. Kofi betrayed you. She has your pant. You must sell cursed tomato back to Madam Darkoa on Tuesday market day, when market fullest, when queen mother Queen Yaa is there. If she eats her own trap, power will break.\n\nNARRATOR: Tuesday, Kaneshie full like Christmas, thousands market women. Queen Yaa, 45 years, market queen mother, inspecting stalls. Ama with fear, holding cursed tomato wrapped in white handkerchief blessed by Mallam Musah, goes to Madam Darkoa stall.\n\nAMA MENSAH: Madam Darkoa, good morning. I want to sell you tomato. Very fresh. From my farm. Better than yours. Sweeter. Taste and see.\n\nMADAM DARKOA: You small girl want to sell tomato to tomato seller? Okay, give me. Let me taste your poor tomato. AHHH! AHHH! WHAT HAVE YOU DONE!\n\nNARRATOR: Green lightning explodes from Madam Darkoa mouth, eyes, hands! Throat glows green, skin cracks like old rotten tomato, blue Kente turns black. All trapped destinies fly out as bright green lights and return to owners. Market women who poor suddenly get customers. Girl who couldn't marry for 7 years gets proposal same day.\n\nMADAM DARKOA: If I go down, you go down with me!\n\nAMA MENSAH: I reject you in Jesus name and Allah name! Kaneshie market is for hard work, not witchcraft!\n\nGRANDMA ESI: Return to where you came from, witch!\n\nNARRATOR: GBOOOM! Madam Darkoa melts into pile of rotten tomatoes, green smoke to sky. Only her stall burns with green fire.\n\nQUEEN YAA: From today, Madam Darkoa spot given to Ama Mensah for bravery! We pray every Monday!\n\nKOFI: Ama, forgive me, I was greedy.\n\nAMA MENSAH: I forgive you brother, but return GHC 5000 to Mallam Musah for cleansing.\n\nNARRATOR: One month later, Ama biggest tomato business in Kaneshie and Agbogbloshie. Mr Joseph, rich businessman, gives her cold store. She marries Mr Joseph son, kind man. At night, when Ama closes stall, sometimes she still sees one green tomato glowing under Madam Darkoa old table... She never touches it. THE END.\n\nMORAL: Not every shining business clean. Hard work with small profit better than quick money that steals future.",
